> Hi Bob!
>
> Do you have in mind a simple include facility for Stan programs and C++? Or are you even opting for a well-defined API on the C++ side which is intended to stay stable over time? This would be very ambitious, I think.

Yes.  That's the plan.  It shouldn't be too hard if we
keep the include functionality simple.  I don't want to
write an entire preprocessor.

> If I get your words right, then my Stan only solution should performance wise be very similar to Thorsten. Would be interesting to benchmark this.

If Torsten's not computing analytic derivatives, then it just
comes down to memory locality and reusing shared computations
efficiently.

> My main thought of going a Stan only solution was simple - you made a commitment to the Stan language and as such I can be sure that my programs will keep working for the foreseeable time. Anything under the hood appears to be under constant refactoring which is how a good software evolves over time.

Right.  We're trying to stabilize the lower-level bits more.
I think the math library's structure will be stable longer term
and so will stan-dev/stan after Daniel finishes the algorithm/command
refactor and we clean up the model class interface some more.

- Bob

